    content: "The Adventist understanding of salvation resists two opposite errors that have plagued Christian history: the idea that human effort contributes to justification, and the idea that grace renders obedience irrelevant. The biblical passages in this lesson guard against both.\n\n**Grace as the sole basis.** When Paul writes \"not by works of righteousness which we have done\" (Titus 3:5), he eliminates every form of merit-based salvation. Adventists affirm without reservation that justification is by grace alone through faith alone in Christ alone. The 1888 Minneapolis General Conference session — where E. J. Waggoner and A. T. Jones pressed the centrality of righteousness by faith — stands as a landmark recovery of this emphasis within the movement. The gospel is not \"grace plus Sabbath-keeping\" or \"faith plus dietary reform\"; it is Christ's righteousness credited to the believer who trusts Him.\n\n**New creation and the Great Controversy.** The language of 2 Corinthians 5:17 resonates with the cosmic conflict theme. Satan's strategy from Eden forward has been to convince humanity that God's character is untrustworthy and His law is oppressive. The new creation demonstrates the opposite: God's love is so extravagant that He enters the sinner's condition to bring the sinner into His own righteousness. Every converted life is a living rebuttal of Satan's accusations against the divine character.\n\n**The sanctuary and propitiation.** Paul's use of hilasterion in Romans 3:25 deliberately evokes the mercy seat in the Most Holy Place (the same Greek word appears in Hebrews 9:5 for the mercy seat). For Adventists, the sanctuary is not an abstract theological category but the interpretive framework for understanding how atonement works — sacrifice, mediation, and judgment are interconnected realities, all centered in the person and ministry of Christ.\n\n**The Spirit's role in regeneration.** Titus 3:5-6 underscores that salvation is not merely a legal transaction; it involves inward transformation by the Holy Spirit. Adventists hold that justification (God's declaration of the sinner's new legal status) and regeneration (God's creation of a new heart) occur together at conversion. The declared status and the experienced reality belong to the same saving act.",
