    content: "Ephesians 2:1-10 presents the most comprehensive single-passage portrait of salvation's practical shape. Paul begins with a diagnosis: humanity was not merely sick but dead — \"dead in trespasses and sins.\" Dead people do not contribute to their own resuscitation. The action belongs entirely to God, who is \"rich in mercy.\" The verbs that follow are all divine actions: He made alive, He raised up, He seated us in heavenly places. Salvation is something done to us and for us before it is ever something done through us.\n\nYet Paul does not stop at rescue. Verse 10 pivots to purpose: \"We are His workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works.\" The Greek word poiema (from which we get \"poem\") suggests a crafted masterpiece. Good works are not the cause of salvation but its intended product. God designed specific paths of service for each believer before they were born. Living the saved life means walking into those prepared works — not manufacturing righteousness but inhabiting what grace has already arranged.\n\nColossians 1:13-14 uses the language of regime change. Salvation is not a minor adjustment within the same system; it is a transfer of citizenship from one kingdom to another. The \"power of darkness\" is not a vague spiritual atmosphere but a dominion with real authority over its subjects. Christ's redemptive act physically relocates the believer from one jurisdiction to another. The forgiveness of sins is not simply the removal of guilt but the legal basis for the transfer — without it, the old kingdom retains its claim.\n\n1 John 2:1-2 addresses the practical reality that saved people still sin. John's purpose is dual: he writes \"so that you may not sin\" — the goal is holiness — but he immediately provides for failure: \"if anyone sins, we have an Advocate.\" The present tense of \"we have\" indicates an ongoing ministry, not a one-time event. Christ's advocacy before the Father means that the believer who stumbles is not abandoned to the old kingdom but represented by the righteous One whose propitiation covers all sin.",
