    content: "The Adventist community has wrestled with each of these misunderstandings, and the biblical correctives in this lesson illuminate the path forward:\n\n**Against cheap grace.** Some critics accuse Adventists of legalism because of their emphasis on the law, Sabbath, and lifestyle standards. But Romans 6:1-4 shows that the real danger runs in the opposite direction: a grace that asks nothing is a grace that changes nothing. Adventists insist that genuine conversion produces visible transformation — not as the basis of acceptance, but as the evidence of new life. The believer who has died with Christ in baptism walks in \"newness of life,\" and that newness has observable, practical dimensions.\n\n**Against dead orthodoxy.** James's warning strikes close to home for any tradition that values doctrinal precision. It is possible to hold all twenty-eight fundamental beliefs intellectually while remaining unchanged in character. Adventist faith is not merely a list of correct propositions but a living trust that reshapes priorities, relationships, and daily habits. The danger for doctrinally rigorous communities is assuming that knowing the right answers is the same as having living faith.\n\n**Against legalism.** Galatians 5:1 is the standing rebuke to every form of spiritual bondage that creeps into religious communities — the unwritten rules, the cultural expectations masquerading as divine requirements, the subtle belief that God's acceptance depends on perfect performance. Adventists must continually distinguish between obedience as a response to grace and obedience as a condition for grace. The first produces joy and freedom; the second produces anxiety and burnout.\n\n**Faith working through love.** Paul's summary in Galatians 5:6 — \"faith working through love\" — provides the Adventist integration point. Sabbath-keeping motivated by love for the Creator is worship. Health reform motivated by love for the body God gave is stewardship. Evangelism motivated by love for the lost is mission. When love is the engine, obedience ceases to be burden and becomes delight. The law becomes what the psalmist always said it was: the expression of God's character, cherished by those who know Him.\n\n**Baptism and the Great Controversy.** Romans 6:3-4 gives baptism a cosmic significance. The public act of immersion — going under the water as Christ went into the grave, rising as Christ rose — is a declaration before the watching universe that this person has changed allegiances. In the Great Controversy framework, every baptism is a victory for Christ's kingdom and a loss for the enemy's domain.",
